# 0008. Codex calls go through one tested launcher

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-25

Claude's read-only Codex calls (ask, continue, review) go through one stdlib-Python script
that sets the safety flags itself, so the read-only guarantee from
[0006](0006-codex-read-only-second-agent.adr) is code with a self-test, not a table Claude
retypes on each call. Approved by the owner on 2026-11-25.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the `codex` skill on `main` lists four commands with different spellings of
  the sandbox flag and no approval policy. On 2026-11-25 that let Codex's auto-reviewer
  (`approvals_reviewer = "auto_review"` in my config) approve a write out of `-s read-only`.
  PR #22 (open) adds `approval_policy="never"` and `--skip-git-repo-check` to the table, but
  it is still a table: nothing tests it, and every new caller can get it wrong again.
- **Decision**: one launcher, `plugins/workflow/skills/codex/codex.py`, the only way Claude
  makes read-only Codex calls. Claude runs it from any working directory as
  `python3 -I <codex skill dir>/codex.py <cmd> ...`. The skill's command table becomes these
  subcommands. The docs command stays as PR #22 leaves it until
  [0009](0009-codex-proposes-docs-as-file-text.adr) replaces it in its own PR.
- **Subcommands**:
  - `ask [--repo DIR]`: new thread, `codex exec --json ... -`. The prompt is read from the
    launcher's stdin and written byte-for-byte to the child's stdin, never put on a command
    line. Claude sends it with a quoted heredoc whose delimiter is `CODEX_PROMPT_END`,
    a string the skill tells Claude never to put inside a prompt. `--repo DIR` runs Codex
    with `DIR` as its working directory (repo questions read that repo's files and
    `AGENTS.md`; `exec resume` has no `-C`, so the launcher sets the cwd for both). Every call passes
    `--skip-git-repo-check`: it grants nothing, and lets `--repo` name a folder that isn't a
    git repo.
  - `resume THREAD_ID [--repo DIR]`: `codex exec resume --json ... THREAD_ID -`, same stdin
    and `--repo` handling. `THREAD_ID` must match a UUID regex, so it can never be parsed as
    an option.
  - `review [--base REF]` (default `origin/main`), run from the repo root: fetches the
    remote and branch named in `REF`, resolves `base` and `HEAD` with `git rev-parse`, runs
    `codex review --base <base-sha>`, then checks `HEAD` is unchanged. Only on success does it
    print `Codex review of <base-sha>..<head-sha>` first; Claude copies that line into the
    PR's `## Codex review`, so a review of an older commit is visible.
- **Fixed flags, every call**: `-c sandbox_mode="read-only"`, `-c approval_policy="never"`,
  `-c mcp_servers={}` (0.172.0 accepts it; no MCP servers or Codex plugins are installed
  today, and this keeps a future one out of Claude's calls), and `--disable` for the
  built-in features whose tools act outside the sandbox: `apps`, `plugins`, `remote_plugin`,
  `browser_use`, `browser_use_external`, `computer_use`, `image_generation`,
  `skill_mcp_dependency_install`, `tool_suggest`, and `hooks` (user hooks run on the host,
  outside the sandbox). The live probe found that `apps` alone
  gave a "read-only" session ChatGPT connector tools that deploy sites, trash pages and change
  sharing, settings and env vars; `mcp_servers={}` doesn't remove them. No command-line option can
  remove or change them. If Codex rejects any of them, the call fails, and the launcher
  reports it as skipped (fails closed). The script has no way to pass
  `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`, `--full-auto`, `workspace-write` or
  `danger-full-access`.
- **Output contract, all three subcommands**: success prints `thread: <id>` (ask/resume) or
  the review header, then Codex's final message, nothing else. Any failure (not installed,
  `codex login status` not logged in, fetch or `rev-parse` failure, non-zero exit, no final
  message, `HEAD` moved) prints only `Codex: skipped (<reason>)`, one line of at most 200
  chars with home paths shortened to `~`, and exits 3. Reasons are fixed text (`codex exited 1`,
  `git fetch failed`), never child stderr: Codex echoes the prompt there, and git can print a
  remote URL with credentials, and this line goes into PR bodies. Bad arguments give the same
  skip line. `review` also refuses uncommitted tracked changes before and after the call,
  because `codex review --base` reviews the working tree, and the header must name what was
  reviewed. A remote base is fetched as `refs/heads/<branch>` (name checked, no `:` or leading
  `-`) into a per-run ref (`refs/codex-review/<pid>`, deleted after), so a concurrent fetch
  can't swap the base. That is the line
  [0007](0007-where-codex-runs-in-the-flow.adr) asks Claude to record.
- **Self-test** (`--test`, per [0004](0004-stdlib-python-scripts-with-self-tests.adr)), in
  `make check`: a fake `codex` first on `PATH` records argv and stdin and replays canned
  JSONL. It asserts that every subcommand gets all fixed flags, the prompt reaches stdin
  unchanged (including quotes, backticks and `$`), `--repo` sets the cwd, a non-UUID thread
  id is refused, the thread id and final message are parsed, and each failure gives the skip
  line and exit 3. Review runs in a throwaway git repo and checks its header and the
  moved-`HEAD` case.
- **Live probe**, recorded in the PR (CI has no Codex): in a temp dir the caller can write
  to, ask Codex to create a file through `ask`, through `resume` of a thread started with
  default settings, and through `review` with a prompt that asks it to. Each must fail with
  "Operation not permitted" and leave the file absent. Before the probe, a control `touch`
  by the caller itself must succeed, so a parent sandbox isn't what blocks the write. Also
  record which tools Codex reports having in each mode.
- **Files**: new `codex.py`; `codex` skill command table and wording; `make check` line;
  workflow minor bump; CHANGELOG; CLAUDE.md layout line. Callers (`github-flow`,
  `security-audit`, `adr-manager`) already defer to the `codex` skill and stay unchanged.
  This replaces the command list in 0006 for these three calls; 0006's decision stands.
- **Consequences**: one place to fix when Codex's CLI changes, safety flags tested in CI, no
  shell quoting in prompts, reviews tied to commits. Cost: ~150 lines to maintain. If Codex
  changes its JSONL event format, parsing breaks: the canned events in the self-test are the
  reference, and the skip path keeps work unblocked.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Python 3 stdlib (`subprocess`, `json`, `argparse`, `re`), Codex CLI 0.172.0, git.
- Alternatives:
  - Command table, as fixed by PR #22: correct today, but untested, and it has already failed
    once.
  - A named profile in `~/.codex/config.toml` selected per call: lives outside this repo, so
    every machine has to set it up and CI can't test it. Callers would still have to remember
    the profile flag.
  - Shell wrapper: JSON parsing and tests are awkward (why 0004 chose Python).
  - Push hook that refuses a push without a review header for the current `HEAD`: stronger
    enforcement, but a separate decision. Revisit if the header proves insufficient.
